### Early October 2026 — Finding and downloading the dataset

I downloaded the **Wearable Device Dataset from Induced Stress
and Structured Exercise Sessions**, available through PhysioNet.

This dataset fits my project because it includes both mental
stress tasks and physical exercise recorded with an Empatica
E4 wristband. The files are organized into stress, aerobic
exercise, and anaerobic exercise sessions.

The available signals include heart rate, blood volume pulse,
movement, skin temperature, and electrodermal activity. The
dataset also includes event markers and self-reported stress
levels.

One important part of the analysis will be identifying the
different periods within each recording. A stress session
includes rest periods, so the folder name alone is not enough
to label every measurement.

My next step is to inspect the files, review the documented
recording issues, and begin plotting individual signals.

[View the dataset on PhysioNet](https://physionet.org/content/wearable-device-dataset/1.0.1/)

### September 2026 — Choosing the project

I started this project with a question: can wearable data help
tell the difference between mental stress and physical activity?

I wanted a senior project that would help me develop skills
relevant to the health and wellness industry. This question
gives me an opportunity to work with time-series data,
machine learning, and Python package development.

The paper *Stressed or just running?* by Yekta Said Can helped
shape my thinking about the problem. It examines the challenge
of distinguishing physiological responses to mental stress
and physical activity.

My plan is to begin with an existing research dataset,
understand its signals and labels, and build a baseline model.
I will then turn the useful data-processing steps into
reusable Python functions.

## Analysis plan

My first analysis will focus on:

- Counting participants and recordings in each session type.
- Reviewing missing values and documented recording problems.
- Plotting heart rate and movement for individual participants.
- Using event markers to distinguish task and rest periods.

After preparing the data, I plan to compare classification
models and evaluate them on participants excluded from training.

I will add plots, results, and explanations here as the work
is completed.

